---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../../components/Eyebrow.astro";
import SectionLabel from "../../components/SectionLabel.astro";

const tocItems = [
	{ id: "sec-0", label: "The false trade-off" },
	{ id: "sec-1", label: "What speed buys" },
	{ id: "sec-2", label: "What quality is" },
	{ id: "sec-3", label: "The concordance" },
];

// Essay column typography
const heading = "mb-4.5 font-display text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink";
const prose = "mb-5.5 text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-pretty text-ink-soft";

// Figures bleed to the viewport edge (capped at the 1440px site wrapper):
// full-bleed both sides on mobile, right-only past the 952px column on desktop.
const figBleed =
	"my-[34px] mr-[min(-20px,calc((952px-min(100vw,1440px))/2))] ml-[min(0px,max(-20px,calc(min(100vw,1440px)-992px)))]";
const figMedia =
	"flex h-[clamp(260px,40vw,460px)] items-center justify-center bg-[repeating-linear-gradient(45deg,#1a1a1a,#1a1a1a_8px,#1d1d1d_8px,#1d1d1d_16px)]";
---

<Layout
	title="Speed and quality — Lawrence Job"
	crumbs={[{ label: "Thoughts", href: "/thoughts" }, { label: "Concordance" }]}
>
	<!-- Reading progress -->
	<div id="reading-progress" class="fixed top-0 left-0 z-[60] h-0.5 w-0 bg-copper" aria-hidden="true"></div>

	<!-- Essay header -->
	<header class="mx-auto max-w-[952px] px-5 pt-[clamp(56px,8vw,110px)] pb-[clamp(40px,5vw,64px)]">
		<Eyebrow class="mb-5">Concordance · Essay 02</Eyebrow>
		<h1
			class="max-w-[800px] font-display text-[clamp(38px,5vw,64px)] leading-[1.04] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-balance text-ink"
		>
			Speed and quality
		</h1>
		<p class="mt-6 max-w-[640px] text-[clamp(17px,1.7vw,21px)] leading-[1.6] text-pretty text-ink-soft">
			Teams are often asked to choose between moving fast and building well. I think that’s the wrong question — done
			properly, each one buys you the other.
		</p>
		<div class="mt-7 flex gap-6 text-[13px] text-ink-mute">
			<span>February 2026</span>
			<span>·</span>
			<span>6 min read</span>
		</div>
	</header>

	<!-- Body: sticky contents + prose -->
	<div class="mx-auto flex max-w-[952px] items-start gap-16 px-5">
		<nav class="sticky top-16 hidden w-[210px] flex-none pt-2 min-[960px]:block" aria-label="Contents">
			<p class="mb-4.5 text-[11px] tracking-[0.16em] uppercase text-ink-mute">Contents</p>
			<div class="relative pl-4.5">
				<div class="absolute top-0 bottom-0 left-0 w-px bg-[rgb(245_245_245/0.1)]" aria-hidden="true"></div>
				<div
					id="toc-bar"
					class="absolute top-0 left-0 h-9 w-px bg-copper transition-transform duration-[350ms] ease-[cubic-bezier(0.4,0,0.2,1)]"
					aria-hidden="true"
				>
				</div>
				{
					tocItems.map((item) => (
						<button
							type="button"
							data-target={item.id}
							class="toc-link flex h-9 w-full cursor-pointer items-center text-left text-[13.5px] text-ink-mute transition-colors duration-300"
						>
							{item.label}
						</button>
					))
				}
			</div>
		</nav>

		<article class="min-w-0 max-w-[660px] flex-1">
			<section id="sec-0" class="pt-2">
				<h2 class={heading}>The false trade-off</h2>
				<p class={prose}>
					The trade-off gets presented as a slider: push it one way and you ship sooner, push it the other and you
					ship better. Almost every planning conversation I’ve sat in has this slider somewhere in the room, usually
					unspoken. And almost every time, it’s describing something that isn’t really there.
				</p>
				<p class={prose}>
					What the slider actually measures is <span class="text-ink">how much unfinished thinking</span> you’re
					willing to push downstream. Cut corners and the thinking still has to happen — it just happens later, during
					an incident, inside a review, or in the head of whoever inherits the code. Speed wasn’t bought. It was
					borrowed.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="sec-1" class="pt-9">
				<h2 class={heading}>What speed actually buys</h2>
				<p class={prose}>
					Speed is not the absence of care. Speed is a short feedback loop. A team that ships in small pieces learns
					what’s true about its product every few days, and truth is the raw material of quality. The slowest teams
					I’ve worked with weren’t careful — they were waiting: for reviews, for environments, for permission.
				</p>

				<figure class={figBleed}>
					<div class={figMedia}>
						<span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-mute">photograph — release board / team wall</span>
					</div>
					<figcaption class="flex max-w-[660px] items-baseline gap-4 px-5 pt-4">
						<span class="flex-none text-xs tracking-[0.1em] text-copper tabular-nums">FIG. 01</span>
						<span class="text-sm leading-[1.6] text-ink-mute">
							<span class="font-medium text-ink-soft">Small releases, visible learning.</span>
							{" Every card on this wall is a shipped decision — the loop between “we think” and “we know” measured in days, not quarters."}
						</span>
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<p class={prose}>
					When the loop is short, mistakes are cheap. Cheap mistakes are the precondition for ambitious engineering —
					you can afford to try the better design because being wrong costs you an afternoon, not a quarter.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="sec-2" class="pt-9">
				<h2 class={heading}>What quality actually is</h2>
				<p class={prose}>
					Quality isn’t polish, and it isn’t perfectionism. It’s the property of a system that lets you change it
					without fear. Tests, types, boundaries, good names — they all serve the same end: the next change is easy.
					Which is to say, quality is stored speed.
				</p>
				<p class={prose}>
					This is why the slider is a lie. The teams that move fastest over a year are the ones whose codebase never
					fights back. They didn’t choose quality over speed; they invested speed to make more of it.
				</p>

				<figure class={figBleed}>
					<div class={figMedia}>
						<span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-mute">diagram — change-cost curve over time</span>
					</div>
					<figcaption class="flex max-w-[660px] items-baseline gap-4 px-5 pt-4">
						<span class="flex-none text-xs tracking-[0.1em] text-copper tabular-nums">FIG. 02</span>
						<span class="text-sm leading-[1.6] text-ink-mute">
							<span class="font-medium text-ink-soft">Quality is stored speed.</span>
							{" The cost of the next change, plotted over a year: the “slow, careful” team’s line stays flat while the “fast” team’s climbs until it stalls."}
						</span>
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</section>

			<section id="sec-3" class="pt-9 pb-[clamp(56px,8vw,96px)]">
				<h2 class={heading}>The concordance</h2>
				<p class={prose}>
					So the question is never “fast or good?”. The useful question is: <span class="copper-mark"
						>which corner, if we cut it, will we have to re-cut forever?</span
					> Some corners are genuinely free — cut those with a clear conscience. The rest are loans, and loans have interest.
				</p>
				<p class="text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-pretty text-ink-soft">
					Speed and quality aren’t opposite ends of a slider. They’re the same discipline, measured on different
					days.
				</p>
			</section>
		</article>
	</div>

	<!-- Keep reading -->
	<section>
		<SectionLabel class="mx-auto max-w-[952px] px-5 pb-2">Keep reading</SectionLabel>
		<div
			class="mx-auto grid max-w-[952px] grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr))] gap-2 px-[min(20px,max(0px,calc((min(100vw,1440px)-912px)/2)))] pt-3"
		>
			<a href="#" class="tile flex min-h-[180px] flex-col justify-between gap-6 px-[clamp(20px,3vw,36px)] py-7">
				<Eyebrow>Next in Concordance</Eyebrow>
				<div class="flex items-end justify-between gap-4">
					<span class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Product and engineering</span>
					<span class="text-xl text-ink-soft" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
				</div>
			</a>
			<a
				href="/thoughts"
				class="tile flex min-h-[180px] flex-col justify-between gap-6 px-[clamp(20px,3vw,36px)] py-7"
			>
				<Eyebrow>Series</Eyebrow>
				<div class="flex items-end justify-between gap-4">
					<span class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">All Concordance essays</span>
					<span class="text-xl text-ink-soft" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
				</div>
			</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>

<style>
	.toc-link[aria-current="true"] {
		color: var(--color-ink);
	}
</style>

<script>
	// Reading aids: copper progress bar at the top of the viewport, and a
	// sticky table of contents whose copper bar tracks the section in view.
	const progress = document.getElementById("reading-progress");
	const bar = document.getElementById("toc-bar");
	const links = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(".toc-link")];
	let active = -1;

	function onScroll() {
		let next = 0;
		links.forEach((link, i) => {
			const el = document.getElementById(link.dataset.target ?? "");
			if (el && el.getBoundingClientRect().top < 180) next = i;
		});

		const doc = document.documentElement;
		const max = doc.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
		if (progress) progress.style.width = `${(max > 0 ? Math.min(1, window.scrollY / max) * 100 : 0).toFixed(2)}%`;

		// The last heading may never cross the activation threshold on short
		// viewports — treat reaching the bottom as reaching the last section.
		if (max > 0 && window.scrollY >= max - 2) next = links.length - 1;

		if (next !== active) {
			active = next;
			if (bar) bar.style.transform = `translateY(${active * 36}px)`;
			links.forEach((link, i) => link.setAttribute("aria-current", i === active ? "true" : "false"));
		}
	}

	links.forEach((link) => {
		link.addEventListener("click", () => {
			const el = document.getElementById(link.dataset.target ?? "");
			if (el) window.scrollTo({ top: el.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.scrollY - 72, behavior: "smooth" });
		});
	});

	window.addEventListener("scroll", onScroll, { passive: true });
	onScroll();
</script>
